**CHAPTER I / PLAYING THE GAME**

# Skills and Movement


### Skills

### Helping with Skill Rolls

A character can only help with a skill roll if they are both trained.

### Take 10

Out of combat a character may “take 10” on a skill roll; the task takes 10 times longer than it normally would (usually meaning a 1 round skill check takes 10 rounds or 1 minute) and instead of rolling a D20, the character may just add 10 to their skill check modifier.

### Hide

The DC for hide is changed from 15 to the Passive Perception of the potential observer.

{{note
**Extended tests**

For tasks that take multiple attempts, see pages 4–5.
}}

\column

### Movement

### Climbing and Swimming

Without Athletics training, each foot climbed or swum costs 3 feet of movement. With Athletics training, each foot costs 2 feet. At Speed 30, you can cover 10 or 15 feet respectively, or 20 or 30 feet if you Dash.

### Moving Through Allies

Moving through allies counts as difficult terrain.

### Using a High Jump to Reach

You can extend your arms half your height above yourself during the jump. Thus, you can reach a distance equal to the height of the jump plus 1½ times your height.

### Jumping

Jumping uses movement foot for foot. Your total movement, including the jump, is limited to your Speed, or twice your Speed if you Dash. Movement spent before the jump reduces how far you can jump.


{{wide

### Jump Distances and Heights

| Jump | Base distance or height | Untrained · DC 15 STR | Trained · DC 10 Athletics |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Standing long | ½ Strength score in feet | +1 ft. | +1d3 ft. |
| Running long | Strength score in feet | +1d3 ft. | +1d6 ft. |
| Standing high | ½ (1 + STR modifier) ft., minimum 1 ft. | +1 ft. | +2 ft. |
| Running high | (1 + STR modifier) ft., minimum 1 ft. | +1 ft. | +2 ft. |

Running jumps require a run-up of at least 10 feet. High-jump height measures how far your feet rise above the ground.

}}

{{pageNumber 3}}
\page


**CHAPTER I / SKILLS**

# Extended Tests

{{wide

Use an extended test when a task takes sustained effort and the situation can change before the characters finish. Freeing a jammed floodgate during combat, deciphering a ward before it resets, and searching a marsh for a lost barge all work the same way. The party earns Progress toward a goal while time and setbacks create pressure.

If one roll can settle the outcome, use a normal ability check. If the party has ample time and success is inevitable, tell them how long the work takes.

}}

### 1. Set the Task

Before the first roll, tell the players what success accomplishes, how much Progress they need, how long each attempt takes, and what consequences their characters can foresee.

Start with DC 15, 5 Progress, and 3 Setbacks. Adjust the DC for how hard each attempt is, the Progress goal for how much work there is, and the deadline for how much time the party has. Use a deadline when the danger advances even if nobody works. Use Setbacks when mistakes make the situation worse. A task can use both.

| Setting | Suggested values |
| --- | --- |
| Work interval | 1 round for urgent tasks, 10 minutes for scene-length work, 1 hour or 1 day for projects |
| Check DC | 10 for straightforward work under pressure, 15 for demanding work, 20 for expert work |
| Progress goal | 3 for a short obstacle, 5 for a substantial task, 8 for a major undertaking |
| Setback limit | 3 by default. Use 2 for fragile tasks or 4 for forgiving ones. |
| Deadline | Optional. Set a number of intervals before an outside event occurs. |

\column

### 2. Work One Interval at a Time

One character leads each interval. The players describe what that character does, and the DM calls for an appropriate ability check. The lead can change between intervals, and a new approach can use a different ability or proficiency. For example, a character might use Intelligence (Investigation) to locate a jam, then another might use Strength (Athletics) to force the mechanism free.

One other character can assist. If the helper describes a contribution that could plausibly improve the attempt, the lead rolls with Advantage. Both characters commit the interval to the task. In combat, the lead spends an action to work and the helper spends an action to Help. Declare the lead at the start of the round and resolve the check at the end of the lead’s turn. A helper must act before that check resolves.

### Check Results

| Check result | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| Meets or exceeds the DC | Gain 1 Progress. |
| Beats the DC by 5 or more | Gain 2 Progress instead. |
| Falls short by 1–4 | Gain 1 Setback. |
| Falls short by 5 or more | Gain 1 Setback and suffer an immediate complication. |

{{pageNumber 4}}
\page


**CHAPTER I / SKILLS**

# Resolving Extended Tests

### Complications and Creative Solutions

An immediate complication should change the scene. A pry bar snaps, a foothold crumbles, or a witness demands a concession. Whenever possible, name the risk before the player commits to the roll. Keep the accumulated Progress unless an event in the fiction actually undoes the work.

A spell, tool, or clever plan that completes a concrete part of the task can earn 1 Progress without a roll and uses the interval’s attempt. If it solves the entire problem, end the test. If it simply makes the attempt easier, grant Advantage or lower the DC when appropriate.

### 3. Resolve the Outcome

Reach the Progress goal. The party completes the task immediately.

Reach the Setback limit. Apply the stated consequence. Further work requires a change in circumstances, such as replacing a broken tool, finding another route, or accepting a bargain.

Run out of time. Resolve the deadline event. An attempt in the final interval counts before the event occurs. If work can continue afterward, keep the Progress and Setbacks and play on under the changed circumstances.

Pause the work. Keep both totals while the work remains intact. Any outside deadline keeps running.

For tasks that benefit from partial success, set one milestone in advance. A gate might open wide enough for people at 3 Progress but require 5 for a wagon. If the test ends early, the party still gets any milestone it reached.

\column

### The Sluice at Moorside

A salvage barge has run aground at an old sluice outside Moorside. Its crew is trying to get clear before rising water pins the hull against the stonework.

| Setting | Ruling |
| --- | --- |
| Interval | 1 round |
| DC | 15 |
| Goal | 5 Progress raises the gate enough to float the barge through. |
| Milestone | 3 Progress opens a gap where the crew can reach the bank. |
| Setback limit | At 3 Setbacks, the winch seizes. The party needs another way to lift the gate. |
| Deadline | At the end of round 5, the current pins the barge. Even if the gate opens later, the hull needs to be freed. |

On round 1, a rogue clears the ratchet with thieves’ tools. A fighter uses the Help action to hold the chain steady before the rogue acts. The rogue totals 21, earning 2 Progress. On round 2, the fighter takes over and rolls 12 on Strength (Athletics), adding 1 Setback. On round 3, a character cuts away a rope snarled around the gate track. The DM awards 1 Progress without a roll because that physically clears part of the obstruction.

The crew can now scramble to safety. With two rounds left and two more Progress needed to save the barge, the players have a decision to make.

{{pageNumber 5}}
\page


**CHAPTER I / PLAYING THE GAME**

# Combat and Weapons

### Initiative

Use standard D&D initiative. Roll once at the start of combat.

### Grappling

A grappled creature you carry shares your square. To carry it, your carrying capacity must accommodate the creature, its gear, and your current load. When dragging a grappled creature, the weight requirement applies only if it goes Prone. You must then have enough Strength to drag its weight.

### Shooting into Melee

Ranged weapon and spell attacks into melee incur cover only if their path crosses intervening creatures. One creature grants Half Cover (+2 AC). Multiple creatures grant Three-Quarters Cover (+5 AC). If the attack misses only because of that bonus, roll a new attack against the intervening creature, chosen at random if there are several. Enough intervening creatures may grant Total Cover. The usual rules for making ranged attacks while in melee still apply.

### Healing from 0 Hit Points

Every time a character is knocked to 0 hit points and then healed to 1 or more hit points they gain a level of Exhaustion.

\column


#### New Weapons

### Additional Martial Melee Weapons

| Weapon | Cost | Damage | Weight | Properties | Mastery |
|:-------|:----:|:-------|:------:|:-----------|:-------:|
| Broadsword | 15 gp | 1d8 Slashing | 3 lb. |  | Vex |
| Pollaxe | 20 gp | 1d10 Slashing | 6 lb. | Heavy, Reach, Two-Handed | Cleave |

#### Weapon Descriptions

**Broadsword** Also called an *arming sword* or *knight's sword*.


:

**Pollaxe (Poleaxe)** A polearm resembling a halberd, but with a slightly shorter shaft and a hammer opposite the axe blade instead of a hook.


### Longswords

Longswords gain Finesse but cannot be used for Rogue’s sneak attack.

### Alternative Attacks

Alternative attacks change the damage type and reduce the damage die by one step. A shortsword pommel strike, for example, deals 1d4 Bludgeoning instead of 1d6 Slashing. Tertiary attacks reduce the die by two steps unless a special rule specifies another effect. Weapon Mastery effects do not apply to these strikes. An attack can still require Weapon Mastery, as the halberd’s trip attack does.

These strikes are choices for a normal Attack action. Fighting styles and feats work as usual. For example, Polearm Master still grants its separate 1d4 Bludgeoning butt strike as a Bonus Action after taking a qualifying Attack action.

