**CHAPTER II / SPELLCASTING**

# Casting Checks

{{wide

Use these campaign rules alongside the published 2024 spell descriptions and the separate spell-by-spell results document.

### Make a Casting Check

When you finish casting any spell, roll 1d20 + your Proficiency Bonus + the ability modifier used to cast that spell. Include a magic item bonus to your spell attack rolls, if applicable. The casting DC is 10 + the spell's printed level, with cantrips counting as level 0. Upcasting changes the baseline effect, not this DC.

This is a special homebrew roll, not an attack roll, ability check, or saving throw. Advantage, Expertise, Guidance, and other bonuses to those rolls do not apply. A spell entry explicitly changing a casting check does apply. If an item supplies a spell without a casting ability, use its spell attack bonus, or its save DC minus 8; use +0 if neither exists. A natural 1 triggers the calamity procedure on page 10. On a natural 20, improve a successful result by one band, to a maximum of Wonder; a failed total becomes Normal instead.

### Casting Check Results

| Check | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Natural 1 | The spell fails and spell loss applies. Roll on the calamity table on page 10. |
| Below DC | The spell fails with no magical effect. |
| DC to DC + 8 | Normal: resolve the published 2024 spell exactly as written. |
| DC + 9 to DC + 12 | Surge: resolve the normal spell plus its Surge result. |
| DC + 13 to DC + 16 | Greater: resolve the normal spell plus its Greater result. |
| DC + 17 or more | Wonder: resolve the normal spell plus its Wonder result. |

{{note
**Example · Acid Arrow**

Acid Arrow is level 2, so its DC is 12. With a +5 casting bonus, a roll of 14 totals 19: Normal. A natural 20 totals 25: Greater, then improves to Wonder.
}}

}}

{{pageNumber 8}}
\page



# Casting Costs and Effects

### Costs and Consequences

A completed casting uses its action or Reaction, item charge, and any consumed components even if the check fails. Spell slots are not consumed unless the spell is successfully cast. Rituals use their normal extra time and avoid a spell slot, but still require the check and any consumed materials. Interrupted casting follows the normal 2024 rules instead.

:

**Spell loss on a Natural 1:** After resolving its calamity, you cannot cast that spell again by any means until you finish a Long Rest. A cantrip instead returns after a Short or Long Rest. A natural 1 on Wish also triggers its normal non-duplication stress and permanent-loss roll exactly once, regardless of the calamity result. Permanent loss takes precedence.

:

**Optional Spellburn:** Before rolling, sacrifice 2 current HP and 2 maximum HP per +1 to the casting check, up to your Proficiency Bonus in bonus points. Neither value can fall below 1. This sacrifice is not damage and cannot be reduced, redirected, or paid with Temporary HP. Restore the sacrificed maximum HP only when you finish a Long Rest; it does not itself heal current HP. Spellburn never cancels a natural 1.

:

**Divine casting costs:** The divine casting procedure spends the slot or limited use even on a failed check. See page 11.

### Read Each Spell Table

Use one success row. Surge, Greater, and Wonder each add only their own text to the normal published 2024 effect; they do not accumulate. Apply upcasting and cantrip level scaling first. If a row includes multiple changes, apply all of them.

:

**Keep the baseline restrictions:** Retain targeting, creature eligibility, attack rolls, saves, repeat saves, Immunities, Concentration, component costs, range, and duration unless that row explicitly changes them. A successful casting check never guarantees a hit or failed enemy save. Legendary Resistance still applies. The one-spell-slot-per-turn restriction still applies.

:

**Damage and healing:** Unless a row explicitly says every hit, pulse, or breath, additional damage or healing applies only to the first qualifying instance per target in that casting. Roll added damage with the normal damage, using its normal save and damage type. A miss gains no extra damage unless the baseline already damages on a miss. Normal Critical Hit rules apply to added attack damage dice.

:

**Duration and protection:** A duration increase also increases the maximum Concentration time but never removes Concentration. An unqualified benefit lasts for the spell’s duration. An extra target follows the original targeting rules. Temporary HP never stack; a result explicitly adding to that spell’s own grant increases that single grant. Otherwise use the higher grant. An altered Mirror Image uses its new total duplicate count for all references to duplicates.

:

**Mishaps:** Unless specified otherwise, a mishap affects the caster and is instantaneous. Normal Resistance and Immunity apply. Use your spell save DC for any required save. For a random direction, roll 1d8 for the eight compass directions. If movement is blocked, use the nearest legal unoccupied space.

{{pageNumber 9}}

\page


**CHAPTER II / SPELLCASTING**

# Spell Calamities

### Manifestation

Manifestation. On a successful casting, use the spell's listed sensory signature. It is brief, ordinarily lasting a moment as the spell takes effect; descriptions of an ongoing effect may accompany that effect. It adds no damage, light radius, special sense, creature, or statistical benefit. It neither replaces required components nor makes imperceptible casting perceptible when a feature removes every component. The caster chooses purely descriptive details left open by the entry.

### Corruption

Corruption. This is a lasting physical or supernatural alteration to the caster, not a temporary mishap. It survives rests, ordinary healing, changes of clothing, and attempts to cut it away. Unless an entry expressly states an effect, the alteration is cosmetic and grants no bonus, attack, Resistance, valuable material, or special sense. The GM selects unspecified details to fit the spell. Identical corruptions do not multiply their effects; a repeat spreads or exaggerates the appearance.

### Misfire

Misfire. This is a distinct, immediate malfunction of the attempted spell. Resolve exactly its listed effects; the intended normal spell does not also occur. Misfires require no Concentration and end after their listed durations. A transformation changes only what the entry specifies, not the caster's whole stat block. There is no extra saving throw unless stated; any stated save uses the caster's normal spell save DC. Normal Immunities and Resistances apply.

\column

### Resolve a Natural 1

The spell fails, casting resources are spent, and spell loss applies. Roll 1d6 once, then use the indicated entries for that spell. The original mishap is preserved as a separate entry; do not apply it unless the roll calls for it.

| 1d6 | Calamity |
| --- | --- |
| 1–3 | Original mishap only. |
| 4 | Misfire only. |
| 5 | Corruption only. |
| 6 | Both Misfire and Corruption. No original mishap. |

Recovery. Greater Restoration removes one corruption chosen by its caster, in addition to its normal effect; Wish can remove corruption under its normal rules. This is an explicit homebrew extension. Remove Curse does not remove corruption. A repeated corruption is one alteration for removal purposes. Instantaneous damage and spent resources are not refunded. Apply Wish stress and its permanent-loss roll only once even if the selected entry repeats that instruction.

Existing consequences. Keep the original mishap text and all four successful result bands. The new calamity roll determines which failure consequence occurs; it does not change the casting DC or add a second casting check. These are original DCC-inspired entries, not transcriptions of published DCC material.

{{pageNumber 10}}
\page


**CHAPTER III / DIVINE SPELLCASTING**

# Divine Casting Checks

When a divine caster calls on their deity, the god may grant the spell, magnify it, or answer with a rebuke. Every failed casting makes another rebuke more likely until the caster rests or makes amends.

### Who Rolls

Clerics roll for spells of level 1 or higher cast through their Cleric Spellcasting feature, including domain spells. Paladins use the same rule if their oath is explicitly tied to a deity. A campaign can extend it to another character whose spellcasting feature explicitly draws power from a deity. Cantrips, rituals cast without a slot, spells cast from magic items, and class features such as Channel Divinity and Lay on Hands use their usual rules. If a feature casts an eligible spell directly without a slot, apply this rule only if that feature is designated as divine at your table.

### Casting Procedure

1. Set the casting DC. Use the spell’s casting DC if your spell results table supplies one. Otherwise, use 10 + the spell’s base level. Upcasting leaves the casting DC unchanged.

2. Roll the casting check. Roll 1d20 + proficiency bonus + the spellcasting ability modifier for the feature used. A Cleric uses Wisdom and a Paladin uses Charisma. This is a separate homebrew roll from the spell’s attack roll or the target’s saving throw.

3. Check the natural roll first. Your Disapproval Range starts at 1. If the face of the d20 is equal to or below your current range, the spell fails and you roll on the Deity Disapproval table. Otherwise, compare your modified total to the casting DC.

\column

### Resolve the Casting

The casting attempt uses the normal casting time and expends the spell slot or limited use, plus any components the spell consumes, regardless of the result. A failed check produces no spell effect and increases your Disapproval Range by 1, to a maximum of 5. This increase also applies to a failure that triggered disapproval, after resolving the rebuke. On success, apply the spell’s result tier and resolve its attacks and saves as usual. A missed spell attack or a successful target save does not increase disapproval.

| Casting total | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Below DC | Failure. Increase Disapproval Range by 1, to a maximum of 5. |
| DC to DC + 8 | Normal |
| DC + 9 to DC + 12 | Surge |
| DC + 13 to DC + 16 | Greater |
| DC + 17 or more | Wonder |

Use any tier entry the spell has in your spell tables. If it has no entry for the tier rolled, use its normal D&D spell effect. For an eligible divine spell, the disapproval table takes the place of the spell’s Misfire entry when the natural roll triggers a rebuke.

**Disapproval roll.** Roll as many d4s as the natural d20 result that triggered the rebuke. See pages 12–13.

{{pageNumber 11}}
\page


**CHAPTER III / DIVINE SPELLCASTING**

# Deity Disapproval

{{wide

Roll a number of d4s equal to the natural d20 roll that triggered disapproval and add them together. A natural 1 means 1d4, a natural 3 means 3d4, and a natural 5 means 5d4. As the range widens, the caster risks both more frequent and more severe rebukes. Adapt omens and penances to the deity’s doctrine. If a result has no applicable resource, target, or circumstance, move down to the next applicable result. Repeated results refresh their duration. A single check can suffer at most one disapproval penalty to its roll, using the largest applicable penalty and clearing any one-check penalties it triggered.

### Rebukes 1–11

| Total | Rebuke | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | The God Falls Silent | Your holy symbol goes cold, an altar candle gutters, or you hear a single word of reproach. The failed spell is the full penalty. |
| 2 | Unsteady Prayer | Subtract 1d4 from your next divine casting check before your next Long Rest. Roll the penalty after the d20. |
| 3 | An Offering Is Due | You cannot receive a result above Normal until you spend 10 minutes making a fitting offering or finish a Long Rest. The offering can be symbolic. |
| 4 | The God Demands Witness | You cannot receive a result above Normal until you tell another willing creature what favor you sought and what duty your faith asks of you, or finish a Long Rest. |
| 5 | Faltering Focus | You have Disadvantage on the next Constitution saving throw you make to maintain Concentration before your next Long Rest. |
| 6 | Empty Hands | You cannot use a Spellcasting Focus for divine spells until you spend 10 minutes reconsecrating your focus or finish a Long Rest. You can use the spells’ actual material components. |
| 7 | A Burden Shared | Choose a willing ally who witnessed the failure. Until you spend an action tending to them, aiding them, or giving them something they need, subtract 1d4 from divine casting checks. The penalty ends when you finish a Long Rest. |
| 8 | Withheld Grace | Expend one unused use of Channel Divinity granted by the class that supplied the failed spell. |
| 9 | Silence Before Prayer | Until the end of your next turn, or for six seconds outside initiative, you cannot cast divine spells with verbal components. |
| 10 | The God Claims a Tithe | Expend your lowest-level remaining spell slot. |
| 11 | A Test of Humility | Until you finish a Short or Long Rest, subtract 1d4 from your divine casting checks whenever you cast with no conscious ally within 30 feet. |

}}

{{pageNumber 12}}
\page


**CHAPTER III / DIVINE SPELLCASTING**

# Rebukes and Atonement

{{wide

### Rebukes 12–20

| Total | Rebuke | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 12 | Healing Withheld | Your divine spells cannot restore Hit Points to you until you finish a Short or Long Rest. They can still heal other creatures, and other sources can still heal you. |
| 13 | Prayer Goes Unanswered | You cannot cast the failed spell through the same class or source again until you finish a Short or Long Rest. |
| 14 | Interdict | You cannot receive a result above Normal until you finish a Long Rest or make amends. |
| 15 | Cast Down | You gain 1 level of Exhaustion. It follows the normal recovery rules. |
| 16 | The God Demands Restitution | Your Disapproval Range has a minimum of 3 until you make amends. A Long Rest resets it to 3 while the penance is outstanding. |
| 17 | The Greater Gate Closes | You cannot expend your highest-level spell slots to cast divine spells until you finish a Long Rest or make amends. If you have access to only one slot level, this rebuke instead expends one remaining slot of that level. |
| 18 | The Weight of Judgment | You gain 1 level of Exhaustion, and your Disapproval Range becomes 5. |
| 19 | The God Turns Away | You cannot cast divine spells of level 1 or higher until you finish a Short or Long Rest or make amends. |
| 20 | Atonement Required | Apply The God Turns Away. In addition, your Disapproval Range has a minimum of 4 until you make amends. A Long Rest restores access to your spells and resets the range to 4 while the penance is outstanding. |

}}

### Rest and Atonement

Long Rest. Reset your Disapproval Range to 1, subject to any ongoing minimum from the table. If more than one minimum applies, use the highest.

Make amends. When a rebuke calls for atonement, the DM names a concrete act suited to the deity and the current adventure. A god of mercy might require the caster to treat an enemy’s wounds. A god of burial might ask them to recover remains from a flooded crypt in the Rushmoors and see them properly interred. Completing the act clears the rebuke or rebukes it addresses, including any associated minimum, and reduces the current range by 2, to a minimum of 1 or any outstanding minimum. Expended slots and Exhaustion recover under their usual rules.

\column

### Example of Disapproval

A Cleric with +5 to divine casting attempts a 1st-level spell at DC 11. A natural 5 gives a total of 10. The spell fails, the slot is spent, and the range rises from 1 to 2. On the next attempt, a natural 2 triggers disapproval. The Cleric rolls 2d4, gets a 6, and loses the use of a Spellcasting Focus for divine spells until reconsecrating it or completing a Long Rest. The range then rises to 3. A later natural 15 gives a total of 20, beating DC 11 by 9 and earning a Surge.

